  <ul style="margin-left:20px;">
    <li>Confusing <span style="color:var(--success);">gradable</span> antonyms with <span style="color:var(--success);">complementary</span> ones – students often pick “cold” for “hot” when the sentence demands a binary opposite like “alive‑dead”.</li>
    <li>Over‑reliance on <span style="color:var(--success);">prefixes</span> (un‑, in‑) – not every “un‑” word is an antonym (e.g., “unusual” vs “usual” are not strict opposites). Recognising true semantic opposition avoids this error.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the effect of negation – a phrase like “not insignificant” actually means “significant.” Many candidates miss the double‑negative and select the literal synonym.</li>
    <li>Choosing a synonym that fits the word but not the <span style="color:var(--success);">sentence tone</span> – “slim” vs “thin” may both mean “narrow,” but “slim” carries a positive connotation unsuitable for a negative context.</li>
    <li>Memorising isolated word pairs without context – this leads to mis‑application when the exam presents a nuanced sentence requiring a specific shade of meaning.</li>
    <li>Rushing through options and overlooking subtle distractors – distractors often share a root (e.g., “protect” vs “protective”) that can mislead hurried readers.</li>
    <li>Failing to review the list of the top 50 high‑frequency pairs – many candidates neglect this strategic list, missing a quick‑gain opportunity.</li>
  </ul>

